# Store all text as LF in the repository; checkout per platform default.
* text=auto

# The Gentoo overlay is the one part of this repo that gets copied out of a
# Windows checkout and executed on a Linux box, by bash and by portage. Both
# treat a trailing CR as part of the token, so a CRLF ebuild fails with errors
# about commands that do exist. Pin these to LF wherever they are checked out.
apps/desktop/build/gentoo/** text eol=lf

# Same rule, same reason. make also keeps a trailing CR as part of the command,
# and a recipe line continued with `\` breaks outright when a CR sits between
# the backslash and the newline. The Linux build is normally driven from WSL
# against this Windows checkout, so without this the file arrives broken in
# exactly the situation it exists for.
Makefile text eol=lf

# A generated file that is committed. The generator always writes LF, and
# `check:licences` compares what it would write against what is on disk — so a
# CRLF checkout made that gate report „stale" on every Windows machine and never
# on CI. The gate no longer compares line endings either; this keeps the working
# copy from flipping between the two on each checkout.
apps/desktop/src/renderer/src/data/licences.json text eol=lf

# pnpm writes its patches with LF and matches them against the package as the
# store holds it, which is also LF. A CRLF checkout would hand pnpm a patch
# whose every line differs from the file it is meant to apply to, and the
# install fails on Windows and nowhere else — the licences.json rule above,
# one directory over.
patches/** text eol=lf

# The two scripts that open with a shebang. The test runner's transform strips
# that line only when it ends in a bare LF, so with CRLF it kept the CR and
# `render-notices.test.mjs` failed to load on every Windows checkout and never
# on CI. Linux reads the same CR as part of the interpreter name.
.github/scripts/audit-toolchain.mjs text eol=lf
apps/desktop/scripts/render-notices.mjs text eol=lf
